---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import BoxTool from "../tools/BoxTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="box">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Three numbers in, a sheet of panels out. Every edge is a finger joint worked out from the
        <strong class="text-slate-300">actual thickness</strong> of your material, with the
        <strong class="text-slate-300">kerf</strong> already taken off every tooth and added to every notch — so the
        parts tap together square instead of arriving 0.3 mm loose. Lid or no lid, hinged or lay-on, compartments
        inside, and a floor that can sit on the ground or stand on a plinth.
    </Fragment>

    <BoxTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </BoxTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The kerf is the whole game</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A laser does not cut along a line, it burns a slot about 0.15 mm wide centred on it. So a
                        finger cut to size comes out a beam narrower and its socket a beam wider: 0.3 mm of slop in
                        every joint, which is the difference between a box you tap together with a mallet and one that
                        falls apart until it is glued and clamped. Here every finger is drawn half a kerf oversize and
                        every notch half a kerf under, so both come out the size they were meant to be. To measure
                        yours, cut a 20 mm square and measure it — what is missing is the kerf.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Measure your sheet</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Every joint is cut exactly as deep as the thickness you type, which makes it the one number
                        here that has to be right. "3 mm" plywood is routinely 2.7 or 3.2, and a box drawn for 3.0 in
                        stock that is 3.2 will not go together at all. Put callipers on the actual sheet, not on the
                        label. The rest of the box follows from it: the fingers default to three times the thickness,
                        which is the width that is strong enough to knock together and short enough not to snap.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Inside or outside</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A box that has to fit a shelf is described from the outside; a box that has to fit its contents
                        is described from the inside. Both are one toggle, and the panel shows you the other figure as
                        you drag — so you never have to add up wall thicknesses in your head, and never discover at the
                        end that six millimetres of it were plywood.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Five answers to "does it have a lid?"</h2>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">No lid</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Four walls and a floor. A tray, a drawer insert, a planter, a parts bin.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Lay-on</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A plate the size of the box that rests on the rim. On its own it is a tile that slides off,
                            so a second plate — the size of the opening less 0.1 mm on every side — is glued centred
                            underneath and drops into it. Its position is engraved on the lid so it can be glued
                            square.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Tray</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A shallow open box that slips over the outside of this one, the way a shoe box does. Cut
                            with the same joints and the same play, so it slides rather than binds.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Hinged</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A clamshell: base and lid are two halves of one outline, so the grain runs on through the
                            joint. See below for why the pivot is where it is.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Closed</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A sixth panel finger-jointed on exactly like the floor. It never opens again — which is
                            what an enclosure, a die or a sealed housing actually wants.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Inset floor</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Separately from the lid: the floor can sit at the very bottom of the walls, or be raised so
                            the walls carry on below it as a plinth. Raised, it passes <em>through</em> the walls on
                            visible through-tenons — somewhere to hide a cable, or four feet to stand on.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Why the hinge pin hangs in the air</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Put a pivot anywhere inside the back wall and the lid's back corner swings <em>down</em> into the
                    box as the front lifts. Every laser-cut flip-top solves that by cutting a clearance arc out of the
                    rim — which is why they all have a notch behind the hinge and why the lid rocks in it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    There is one family of positions where the problem does not arise: <strong class="text-slate-300">behind the back face and above the rim</strong>. From there every point of the lid lies down-and-forward of
                    the pin, so opening it lifts all of them at once. Nothing has to be cut away, and nothing fouls.
                    The catch is that no part of the box reaches that point — it is in mid-air. So the lid's own side
                    walls grow a round knuckle out to it, and a pair of ears screwed to the outside of the box's side
                    walls meet them there. Two plates in two planes with one pin through both: a strap hinge, and the
                    only kind that opens a full quarter turn without a single clearance cut.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Use a length of 3 mm rod, or an M3 screw with a nut on the far side. The ears' mounting holes are
                    already drilled through the box's side walls to match.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Compartments that lift out</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Dividers cross-lap into each other — slots down from the top on one set, up from the bottom on
                        the other — so a grid of them assembles into one piece and drops into the finished box. They
                        are cut to the space inside less the play on each side, and nothing is glued: pull the grid
                        out, cut a different one, and the box is a different sorter.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Nested for your bed</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The panels are packed into rows no wider than the sheet width you set, so what you export is
                        ready to cut rather than something to rearrange first. A part too big for the sheet widens the
                        layout instead of vanishing off it, and the status bar says which. The names on the canvas are
                        a view toggle — they are never cut.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What each format does with it</h2>
                <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-3">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">SVG</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Every outline in cutting red, at true size in millimetres, with the glue positions in
                            engraving green.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">DXF</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Closed polylines colour-coded by operation, which is how LightBurn and every CAM tool
                            groups a job. No curves to reinterpret: the arcs are already flattened to 0.01 mm.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Falcon Design Space</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A native project with the cut and engrave layers already assigned, so it opens ready to
                            run.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Before you commit a whole sheet: cut one corner of one joint — a 30 mm square off the front wall and
                the piece of side wall that meets it — and check the fit. Kerf varies with power, speed, focus and how
                damp the plywood is, and two minutes here saves a box.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
